# CPU Project Rules and Important Information

## Environment basics
- Don't directly run sbt, we do not use sbt.
- Mill build tool is installed and available on the users PATH, do not run it with ./mill. But simply 'mill ...'. Mill is invoked from the repo root; Chisel sources live in `core/`.
- The CPU is implemented in Scala using the Chisel library

## Commands
- Run CPU tests with trace output: `bash tools/run_cpu_with_trace.sh`
  - This generates trace files at:
    - CPU raw trace: `core/trace/cpu_trace.txt` (directory `core/trace/`)
    - Annotated trace: `core/trace/annotated_trace.txt`
- FPGA bitstream for the Tang Nano 4K: `tools/gowin_build.sh` then SRAM-load with `tools/sram_load.sh -b tangnano4k <file.fs>`. Never flash (`-f`).
- Don't try running mill directly for subsetting the test cases either, simply run the tests as mentioned in the first command so we get full trace output.
- Whenever you want to verify the Chisel still compiles, run `mill chisel-blinky.runMain gcd.CPUSubset0` from the repo root (with `CHISEL_CPU_PROJECT_ROOT` pointing at `core/`).

## Important Notes
- Ensure that when adding new instructions the `tools/trace_analyzer.py` script is updated accordingly or traces will stop making any sense.

## Architecture Notes
- This CPU implements a subset of the 2018 RISC5 architecture (Project Oberon), it is NOT RELATED to the RISC-V architecture.
- The ISA is described in the file `core/docs/risc_5_arch.txt`
- The memory map is described in the file `core/docs/memory_map.txt`
- When making references to the ISA, refer to the above link, do NOT make up your own interpretations of the instructions or use our current implementation as the reference!
- The CPU is designed to be synthesized into the Tang Nano 4K FPGA (`nano4k/`)
- Tests are written in Scala using the ChiselTest library, a good example test is `core/src/test/scala/gcd/CPUSubset0Test.scala`
- The RSC format can be understood using the documentation in `core/docs/rsc_format_specification.txt`
